//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros

// D15 closeout: `x509_verify_signature` used to fail closed on ANY
// `ecdsa-with-SHA384`-signed certificate, with an inline comment stating
// that `ecdsa_p256_verify` only ever built a SHA-256 hashing path. That
// gap blocked every real chain with an SHA-384-signed link -- notably
// Cloudflare's publicly-trusted `1.1.1.1:443` chain, whose SSL.com ECC
// intermediate signs with SHA-384 over a P-256 key. Per RFC 5480 SS2 the
// signature hash and the key's curve are INDEPENDENT in X.509, so P-256 +
// SHA-384 is valid and common; only TLS 1.3's signature-scheme list
// couples them.
//
// This test pins a REAL, openssl-generated, openssl-verified
// ecdsa-with-SHA384 chain (self-signed P-256 root + P-256 leaf), so the
// FIPS 186-4 SS6.4.1 truncation in `ecdsa_p256_verify_sha384` (leftmost
// 256 of SHA-384's 384 bits) cannot silently regress. Self-consistency
// would not catch a wrong-end truncation -- an independent implementation
// is required, hence openssl on both the signing and the verifying side.
//
// Fixture generation (2026-08-27), OpenSSL 3.5.6:
//   openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \
//     -sha384 -keyout root_key.pem -out root_cert.pem -days 3650 -nodes \
//     -subj "/CN=sha384-root-test" \
//     -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE" \
//     -addext "keyUsage=critical,keyCertSign,cRLSign"
//   openssl req -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \
//     -keyout leaf_key.pem -out leaf.csr -nodes -subj "/CN=sha384-leaf-test"
//   openssl x509 -req -in leaf.csr -CA root_cert.pem -CAkey root_key.pem \
//     -CAcreateserial -out leaf_cert.pem -days 3650 -sha384 \
//     -extfile leaf_ext.cnf      # CA:FALSE, SAN DNS:sha384-leaf-test.example
//   openssl x509 -in <cert>.pem -outform DER -out <cert>.der
//
// INDEPENDENT confirmation that the chain is genuinely valid (i.e. that a
// rejection here would be this implementation's fault, not the fixture's):
//   openssl verify -CAfile root_cert.pem leaf_cert.pem   ->  leaf_cert.pem: OK
//
// `openssl x509 -noout -text` confirms for BOTH certificates:
//   Signature Algorithm: ecdsa-with-SHA384 (outer and TBS-inner copies)
//   Public Key Algorithm: id-ecPublicKey, ASN1 OID: prime256v1 (P-256)
//   root: basicConstraints critical CA:TRUE
//   leaf: basicConstraints critical CA:FALSE, SAN DNS:sha384-leaf-test.example
//   Not Before: Aug 27 14:02:03 2026 GMT / Not After: Aug 24 14:02:03 2036 GMT
//
// DER bytes below are the real pipeline output, converted to Sounio byte
// array literals by a script (never retyped by hand). The "bad" leaf flips
// exactly one bit at index 440 of 451, inside the outer
// ECDSA-Sig-Value's `s` INTEGER (the signature BIT STRING's content runs
// 380..450 per `openssl asn1parse`), leaving the TBSCertificate
// and the issuer key byte-identical.

